# Target C-II — fixed FREE pair forces a square-root boundary/forest obstruction without an external-population hypothesis

**Date:** 2026-09-18
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; proof-only strengthening of the fixed-pair/all-run FREE route
**Scope:** the `PAIR-REUSE` branch for the two extreme all-inactive FREE modules
**Dependencies:** fixed-pair lossless common runs; all-run forest-union identity; two-boundary slack tradeoff; two-sided outer-face localization; common-trunk two-normal cone theorem
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_fixed_pair_boundary_forest_trifork_2026_09_18.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The all-run forest-union theorem removes the old `(Z_D+1)^2` run-pair loss when the
number of lower-run components is small. Its remaining correction is the actual number
of lower-run components. Those components are created only by upper gates. But the
upper gates themselves have one bounded total diameter-defect budget. Keeping one
parity of upper ancestry edges gives a large vertex-disjoint near-diameter matching
without spending any extra defect.

This closes the bookkeeping loop and removes the external-population hypothesis from
the fixed-pair `PAIR-REUSE` obstruction.

Fix

\[
h_*:=\frac{\sqrt{303}}{52},\qquad
c_*:=0.129220199818177\ldots,\qquad
b_*:=\sin(c_*/2),
\]

and a fixed `K>h_*`. Put

\[
\alpha_K:=1-\frac{h_*}{K}>0.
\tag{BF.1}
\]

On the `PAIR-REUSE` side of the fixed extreme-pair theorem, the two extreme FREE
ancestries share a set `E_*` of distinct original lower-gate edges with

\[
E:=|E_*|\ge\left(\frac{\alpha_K}{2}-o(1)\right)D,
\tag{BF.2}
\]

and every selected edge satisfies

\[
1<|e|\le1+\frac KD.
\tag{BF.3}
\]

The two canonical support normals `N_1,N_2` obey

\[
c_*-o(1)\le\theta_D:=\angle(N_1,N_2)\le\frac\pi2+o(1).
\tag{BF.4}
\]

The complementary fixed-pair branch already gives a linear vertex-disjoint matching
of inverse-`D` inactive pairs. The theorem below concerns only `PAIR-REUSE`.

## 1. Replace the external-population correction by the actual upper-gate count

Let `U_i` be the number of upper gates on ancestry `i`, and put

\[
T:=U_1+U_2.
\tag{BF.5}
\]

Keep every maximal lower run from either ancestry containing a common edge. Let
`\mathcal P,\mathcal Q` be the resulting path forests, and write `p,q` for their
numbers of components. Deleting the upper gates partitions each ancestry into at most
`U_i+1` lower runs, so

\[
p\le U_1+1,\qquad q\le U_2+1,
\qquad p+q\le T+2.
\tag{BF.6}
\]

Let

\[
H:=\mathcal P\cup\mathcal Q,
\qquad I:=\mathcal P\cap\mathcal Q.
\]

All lower-run edges have length below `sqrt(2)` for large `D`, so `H` is a plane
short-edge source graph. The exact two-forest Euler identity from the all-run theorem is

\[
\beta(H)=c_H-p-q+c_I,
\]

where `c_H,c_I` are the component counts of `H,I`. Since `E_*>0`, `c_H>=1`, and hence

\[
\boxed{
\beta(H)\ge c_I-T-1.
}
\tag{BF.7}

Thus the correction is the actual total number of upper events, not the ambient
external population.

## 2. The same `T` buys an upper matching with bounded total diameter defect

The two-boundary slack theorem gives, separately on each ancestry,

\[
\sum_{e\in E_{\rm up}^{(i)}}(D-|e|)\le t_D,
\qquad
t_D<h_*+D^{-1/3}.
\tag{BF.8}
\]

Let

\[
U:=\max\{U_1,U_2\}\ge T/2.
\]

The upper edges of one ancestry split by ancestry-index parity into two matchings. One
parity class contains at least `U/2-O(1)` upper edges. Keep that class and call it
`M_{\rm up}`. Since its defect sum is at most the sum over all upper edges on that
ancestry,

\[
\boxed{
|M_{\rm up}|\ge\frac U2-O(1)\ge\frac T4-O(1),
}
\tag{BF.9}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{xy\in M_{\rm up}}(D-|x-y|)\le t_D<h_*+D^{-1/3}.
}
\tag{BF.10}
\]

Every member is an original source pair with strict length below `D`, because all
ancestry vertices in this branch are fully inactive in the original source. Every
endpoint is also in the original outer contact face.

This aggregate statement is stronger than first discarding a fixed fraction of upper
edges by Markov: the whole matching has only `O(1)` total diameter deficit.

## 3. A three-way finite minimax

Fix a real threshold `s>1`. Every selected edge of `E_*` belongs to `I`.

If one component `C` of `I` contains at least `s` selected edges (replace `s` by the
appropriate integer ceiling), then the common-trunk cone theorem gives, for one of the
two normalized axes

\[
B=\frac{N_1+N_2}{|N_1+N_2|},
\qquad
T_N=\frac{N_1-N_2}{|N_1-N_2|},
\]

an endpoint displacement `X_C` satisfying

\[
\boxed{
X_C\cdot U_C\ge(b_*-o(1))s,
\qquad U_C\in\{B,T_N\}.
}
\tag{BF.11}

Indeed the SAME-traversal coefficient is `sin(theta_D/2)>=b_*-o(1)`, while the
OPPOSITE-traversal coefficient is
`cos(theta_D/2)>=cos(pi/4)-o(1)>b_*`.

Otherwise every component of `I` contains fewer than `s` selected edges. Then

\[
c_I>\frac Es,
\]

and (BF.7) gives

\[
\beta(H)>\frac Es-T-1.
\tag{BF.12}
\]

At the same time (BF.9) gives an upper matching of size at least `T/4-O(1)`.
For every `T>=0`,

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{\frac Es-T,\frac T4\right\}
\ge\frac{E}{5s}.
}
\tag{BF.13}
\]

The proof is exact: if `T>=4E/(5s)`, the second term is at least `E/(5s)`; otherwise
the first term is larger than `E/(5s)`.

Combining (BF.11)--(BF.13), for every threshold `s` the `PAIR-REUSE` branch forces at
least one of

\[
\beta(H)\ge\frac{E}{5s}-O(1),
\tag{BF.14}
\]

\[
X_C\cdot U_C\ge(b_*-o(1))s,
\tag{BF.15}
\]

or

\[
|M_{\rm up}|\ge\frac{E}{5s}-O(1),
\qquad
\sum_{e\in M_{\rm up}}(D-|e|)\le t_D.
\tag{BF.16}
\]

Choose

\[
s=\sqrt{\frac{E}{5b_*}}.
\tag{BF.17}
\]

Then the two principal terms equal

\[
\sqrt{\frac{b_*E}{5}}.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{PAIR-REUSE}\Longrightarrow
\max\{\beta(H),\ \text{compatible trunk drift},\ |M_{\rm up}|\}
\ge
\sqrt{\frac{b_*E}{5}}-o(\sqrt D),
}
\tag{BF.18}
\]

where in the upper-matching alternative the complete selected matching still satisfies
the bounded aggregate defect (BF.10). No hypothesis on `Z_D=|A\setminus F|` is used.

## 4. Explicit square-root constants

Using (BF.2), define

\[
\boxed{
C_K:=\sqrt{\frac{\alpha_Kb_*}{10}}.
}
\tag{BF.19}
\]

Then the complete fixed-pair fork is:

1. `LINEAR-LOWER`: a vertex-disjoint matching of
   \[
   \left(\frac{\alpha_K}{11}-o(1)\right)D
   \]
   old fully inactive outer-face pairs with `1<|x-y|<=1+K/D`; or
2. `SQRT-LOWER-FACE`: the lower-run union `H` has
   \[
   \beta(H)\ge(C_K-o(1))\sqrt D;
   \]
3. `SQRT-LOWER-TRUNK`: one compatible common lower trunk has actual two-normal axis
   displacement at least
   \[
   (C_K-o(1))\sqrt D;
   \]
4. `SQRT-UPPER`: a vertex-disjoint matching of
   \[
   (C_K-o(1))\sqrt D
   \]
   old fully inactive outer-face pairs satisfying the aggregate diameter-defect bound
   \[
   \boxed{
   \sum_{xy\in M_{\rm up}}(D-|x-y|)<h_*+o(1).
   }
   \tag{BF.20}
   \]

For `K=1`,

\[
\alpha_1=0.6652520156628998\ldots,
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
C_1=0.0655378523927624\ldots.
}
\tag{BF.21}

Thus the fixed extreme FREE pair, without any external-population restriction, forces
one of a linear inverse-`D` lower matching, `0.0655378... sqrt(D)` bounded faces,
`0.0655378... sqrt(D)` actual common-trunk drift, or `0.0655378... sqrt(D)` disjoint
near-diameter pairs whose *total* additive deficit from `D` is below
`0.334748+o(1)`.

## 5. Uniform-slack upper submatchings

The aggregate form (BF.20) can be converted to a uniform threshold without changing
its scale. Let `Lambda>1` be fixed. If `M_{\rm up}` has `M` edges and total deficit at
most `t_D`, fewer than `M/Lambda` edges can have deficit greater than

\[
\frac{\Lambda t_D}{M}.
\]

Therefore the `SQRT-UPPER` branch contains a submatching of size

\[
\boxed{
\left((1-\Lambda^{-1})C_K-o(1)\right)\sqrt D
}
\tag{BF.22}
\]

whose every edge satisfies

\[
\boxed{
D-|x-y|
\le
\left(\frac{\Lambda h_*}{C_K}+o(1)\right)D^{-1/2}.
}
\tag{BF.23}
\]

For `K=1,Lambda=2`, this gives at least

\[
(0.0327689261963812\ldots-o(1))\sqrt D
\]

vertex-disjoint pairs with

\[
D-|x-y|
\le
(10.21540902289523\ldots+o(1))D^{-1/2}.
\]

The aggregate statement BF.20 is preferable whenever a later argument can use a total
slack ledger rather than an edgewise threshold.

## 6. Supercritical upper counts automatically produce a crossing subbraid

There is a further strengthening away from the critical `sqrt(D)` scale. Suppose one
of the two extreme ancestries has `U` upper gates with

\[
U/\sqrt D\to\infty.
\]

Using the preceding Markov argument on one parity gives a matching of `Omega(U)`
original edges of length

\[
D-O(1/U).
\]

Apply the existing bounded-conflict crossing-extraction theorem for a matching of
edges of length at least `L`. Its conflict degree is

\[
O\!\left(1+D(D-L)\right)=O(1+D/U).
\]

Hence the upper matching contains a pairwise properly crossing submatching of size

\[
\boxed{
\Omega\!\left(\frac{U}{1+D/U}\right)
=
\Omega\!\left(\frac{U^2}{D+U}\right).
}
\tag{BF.24}
\]

In particular, if `U=omega(sqrt(D))`, this crossing submatching diverges. This does not
improve the critical square-root branch, where the guaranteed crossing submatching can
remain bounded.

## 7. What remains

This theorem removes the last `Z_D` assumption from the fixed-pair heavy-reuse
bookkeeping. A large external population is no longer needed to absorb the forest
component correction: the actual upper gates that create those components are charged
directly at the opposite metric boundary.

It still does not close C-II. The surviving source-level obstructions are now all
explicit and at least square-root scale:

- a linear inverse-`D` outer-face lower matching;
- square-root many bounded faces in a plane union of two inactive lower-run forests;
- a square-root common lower trunk with actual Euclidean drift in a two-normal axis;
- or a square-root near-diameter matching with bounded total diameter deficit, with a
  diverging crossing subbraid whenever the upper count is supercritical.

The missing step is to convert one of these source-level objects into the canonical
loaded-hull/outer-walk currency, an exact diameter anchor, or a source-preserving strict
competitor. Further external-population pigeonholing is not needed for this fixed-pair
route.

C-II and Level I remain open. No theorem-state authority is changed.

**Disposition:** coupled the exact two-forest Euler correction to the same ancestries'
upper-gate defect budget; kept an entire upper-edge parity matching rather than paying a
Markov loss; solved the resulting three-way minimax; removed every external-population
assumption from the fixed extreme-pair heavy-reuse obstruction; and quantified the
critical near-diameter aggregate-defect branch.
